(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-vignettes"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with chest pain after a car accident that killed her sister has ST elevation and an elevated troponin; stress cardiomyopathy is suspected. Her pain has eased and she is hemodynamically stable in the emergency department. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Admit to cardiology","Discharge home today","Outpatient follow-up only","Refer for transplant listing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Even though her pain has eased and she is stable, Takotsubo mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences, so she is evaluated and treated on established acute coronary syndrome protocols and admitted to cardiology. Treatment is then empiric and supportive, and most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy comes to clinic with mild breathlessness on exertion. Her examination shows trace ankle edema and clear lungs. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 60 pg/mL. What does this natriuretic peptide result indicate?","choices":["Myocarditis is confirmed","Filling pressure is raised","Heart failure is unlikely","Heart failure is confirmed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) is released when ventricular filling pressures are high, and a level below 100 pg/mL excludes heart failure. Her value of 60 pg/mL, with clear lungs and only trace edema, makes heart failure an unlikely cause of her mild exertional breathlessness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has stable dilated cardiomyopathy of two years' duration with no systemic illness and no recent change in his heart function. He has read about heart biopsies online and asks whether he should have one. What is the role of a heart muscle biopsy for him?","choices":["Not routine and rarely useful","Required to confirm every case","The gold standard for his disease","The first test before echocardiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endocardial biopsy is not used routinely in dilated cardiomyopathy and is rarely useful. Its indications are recent onset of rapidly deteriorating cardiac function or a history of systemic disease that can involve the heart, and this man has stable disease with neither. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has newly diagnosed hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with a septum of 2.2 cm and occasional palpitations. Her cardiologist wants to judge her risk of ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death. Which test assesses that risk?","choices":["A repeat chest radiograph","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring","Cardiac catheterization","A genetic testing panel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ambulatory electrocardiogram monitoring for 24 to 48 hours should be performed in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy to assess the risk of ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death, the question her cardiologist is asking. Antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone are then used for sudden death prevention. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is recovering in hospital from stress cardiomyopathy that followed news of her son's serious illness. Her chest pain has resolved, and she asks whether her heart will stay damaged. Which answer about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Poorest outlook of any type","Most patients recover completely","Half die within five years","It always progresses to failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with Takotsubo cardiomyopathy completely recover. Care in hospital is empiric and supportive, with beta blockers, anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present and diuretics if heart failure is present, so she can be told her heart is expected to recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man admitted with presumed viral myocarditis keeps deteriorating despite full medical treatment. Coronary angiography is normal, and valvular, toxic and ischemic causes of heart failure have been excluded. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum troponin","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization","Chest radiograph"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard and is used to establish the diagnosis once other causes of heart failure are excluded, as his have been. It is recommended in acute deterioration of heart function of unknown origin that is not responding to medical treatment, which describes his course. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman received mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy for a chest cancer 15 years ago. She now has progressive exercise intolerance, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), ascites and swollen legs. Echocardiogram shows stiff ventricles of normal size, marked atrial enlargement and abnormal diastolic function with a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy are treatment-related causes of restrictive cardiomyopathy, with chemotherapy leading to inflammation, then fibrosis and restriction. These patients may have a prior history of radiation or chemotherapy and often present at an advanced stage, as she does with stiff ventricles and big atria. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with acute myocarditis after a viral illness has fluid retention with bilateral leg edema and crackles, but his blood pressure is stable. He is receiving supportive care. Which medication may be added as needed for his fluid retention?","choices":["A thrombolytic drug","Alcohol septal ablation","A diuretic","A positive inotrope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care is the mainstay of myocarditis treatment, and medications are added as needed for the clinical scenario. For his fluid retention, with leg edema and crackles, that means a diuretic; vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulation are the other as-needed options. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 23-year-old previously healthy man has breathlessness, chest pain and exercise intolerance ten days after a flulike illness with fever, myalgia and diarrhea. He is tachycardic with a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) and crackles at both bases. Troponin is elevated and coronary angiography is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral myocarditis","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A previously healthy young man with heart failure symptoms ten days after fever, myalgia and diarrhea fits viral myocarditis, which follows a recent (1 to 2 week) flulike syndrome; young males are particularly susceptible. His tachycardia, third heart sound and crackles match the examination. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy developed severe asthma symptoms when a beta blocker was started, and it was stopped. She still has exertional breathlessness from outflow obstruction. Which medication is the appropriate alternative?","choices":["Nifedipine, a vasodilator","Nitroglycerin paste","Norepinephrine infusion","Verapamil or diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the beta blocker stopped because of her asthma, the other listed medication for outflow tract obstruction is a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, verapamil or diltiazem. Like a beta blocker, it is a negative chronotrope and negative inotrope. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has recurrent palpitations from atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter. She already takes anticoagulation to prevent embolic stroke. Which medication is used to convert her rhythm to sinus and suppress recurrence?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Epinephrine","Amiodarone","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is very effective at converting atrial fibrillation and flutter to sinus rhythm and suppressing recurrence, and it reduces the incidence and risk of sudden cardiac death with or without outflow obstruction. Her anticoagulation already covers stroke prevention; radiofrequency ablation is another option. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 77, 78"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has crushing chest pain and breathlessness the day after her husband's funeral. She has no hypertension, diabetes or smoking history. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation with T-wave inversion, troponin is elevated, and echocardiogram shows hypokinesis of the mid and apical segments of the left ventricle. Catheterization shows normal coronary arteries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A postmenopausal woman with chest pain the day after her husband's funeral, no cardiac risk factors, mid and apical hypokinesis and normal coronary arteries has Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy: transient regional systolic dysfunction that mimics acute coronary syndrome without angiographic coronary disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has months of progressive breathlessness, abdominal swelling and bilateral pedal edema. He prefers to sit upright. Jugular venous pressure is raised and he has a Kussmaul sign (jugular venous pressure rises with inspiration), hepatomegaly and ascites. His electrocardiogram shows low voltage (small complexes) in all leads, and echocardiogram shows normal-sized ventricles, marked biatrial enlargement and a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided congestion with normal-sized ventricles, marked biatrial enlargement and a preserved ejection fraction is restrictive cardiomyopathy: impaired diastolic filling with preserved chamber size, both atria enlarged and right heart failure often more prominent. Amyloidosis is the most common cause in the United States. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy has breathlessness on exertion and peripheral edema. On examination her neck veins distend when firm pressure is held over her right upper abdomen, a sign called hepatojugular reflux (neck-vein distension with firm pressure over the liver). Which additional finding is expected with this degree of congestion?","choices":["Congestive enlargement of the liver","A small, soft, nonpalpable liver","A pulsatile mass in the epigastrium","Flank bruising from bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alongside hepatojugular reflux, the gastrointestinal findings of dilated cardiomyopathy include congestive hepatomegaly and ascites. They reflect venous congestion, which also gives jugular vein distention, an enlarged liver and spleen, and dependent pitting edema like hers. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure, ascites and a raised jugular venous pressure has cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to decide between restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis. Which imaging finding would point to constrictive pericarditis instead?","choices":["Marked atrial enlargement","Preserved ejection fraction","Normal ventricular cavity size","Pericardial thickening"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography alone cannot separate restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis, so cardiac magnetic resonance imaging is used, and pericardial thickening points to pericarditis. Big atria, a preserved ejection fraction and normal cavity size are restrictive features that do not decide the question. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy comes to the emergency department with exertional chest pressure. His electrocardiogram shows left ventricular hypertrophy without acute changes, and a clinician reaches for a chest pain order set. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Verapamil","Amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, drugs that reduce preload or afterload, such as nitrates, may increase symptoms by aggravating outflow tract obstruction, so nitroglycerin from a chest pain order set should be avoided if possible. A beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication for his obstruction instead. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man is newly diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy caused by hemochromatosis, with iron deposited in the heart tissue. He has congestion and fatigue. Which is the first principle of his management?","choices":["An inotrope to raise output","A high-intensity exercise program","Treat the underlying disorder","Septal myectomy for relief"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of restrictive cardiomyopathy begins with treating the underlying disorder, here hemochromatosis. Diuretics are then used with caution for his congestion, beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem may improve filling, and anticoagulants or a pacemaker are added as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"An 86-year-old woman with end-stage cardiomyopathy has breathlessness at rest, edema and fatigue despite maximal tolerated therapy. She is not a transplant or device candidate and says she wants comfort at home. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop all of her symptom medicines","Add an assist device urgently","Discuss goals of care and hospice","Refer for heart transplant listing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has end-stage cardiomyopathy, is not a transplant or device candidate and wants comfort at home. Palliative care covers symptom management of dyspnea, fatigue and edema, advance care planning and goals-of-care discussions, caregiver support and hospice referral in end-stage disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy confirmed by echocardiography has typical findings and no plans for surgery or a device. A colleague suggests cardiac catheterization to complete the work-up. When is cardiac catheterization indicated in this condition?","choices":["Always, to rule out coronary disease","Only if the murmur is very loud","Only if surgery or a pacemaker is planned","Always, to confirm the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization is not required for diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; it is obtained when invasive modalities of therapy, a pacemaker or surgery, are planned. With his diagnosis already confirmed and no such plans, it adds nothing now. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has breathlessness on exertion and occasional palpitations. His father has hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. His pulse shows pulsus bisferiens (a double systolic peak), the apical impulse is forceful and sustained, and a fourth heart sound (a late diastolic sound just before the first heart sound) is present. Which is the typical diagnostic test of choice?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Genetic testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is the typical diagnostic test of choice for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, confirming it when unexplained left ventricular wall thickness is 1.5 cm or more. It also shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy, a small cavity, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve and left atrial enlargement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has progressive breathlessness on exertion, fatigue and swelling of both legs. The apical impulse is displaced laterally, a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) is present, and there is a soft murmur at the apex. Which echocardiogram finding would support dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Midsegment and apical hypokinesis alone","Dilated thin walls with a reduced ejection fraction","A thick septum and a small left ventricular cavity","Normal cavity size with large atria and stiff walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His displaced apical impulse, third heart sound and apical murmur fit dilated cardiomyopathy, and the supporting echocardiogram shows dilated, thin walls (the prominent feature) with an ejection fraction below 40%. Hypokinetic chambers, dilation of up to all four chambers, mitral or tricuspid regurgitation and sometimes thrombus may also appear. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is newly diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy after a viral illness. He is in sinus rhythm, blood pressure is 118/72, and his ejection fraction is 30%. No thrombus is seen on echocardiogram. Which pair of drug classes belongs to his guideline-directed medical therapy?","choices":["Anticoagulant and antiarrhythmic started for everyone","Verapamil and a nitrate to relax the thick ventricle","A diuretic alone, until symptoms settle to baseline","Beta blocker and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed medical therapy for adults with cardiomyopathy includes angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta blockers, angiotensin receptor blockers and diuretics, and both drugs in this pair are on the dilated cardiomyopathy drug list. His stable blood pressure, sinus rhythm and ejection fraction of 30% fit starting them. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 42, 76, 98, 105"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is stable on a beta blocker. He drinks six to eight beers on most weekend nights and asks whether he needs to change anything. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["No limit while on his beta blocker","Restrict his daily fluid intake","Restrict salt; alcohol is fine","Avoid drinking alcohol to excess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six to eight beers on most weekend nights is excess, and patient education in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy includes avoiding excess alcohol consumption, alongside maintaining hydration and keeping to mild to moderate, noncompetitive activity. Being stable on a beta blocker does not change that advice. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 76, 82"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with stress cardiomyopathy confirmed by normal coronary arteries at catheterization is stable, with no ventricular thrombus and no signs of heart failure. Which medication is helpful in her supportive treatment?","choices":["An inotrope infusion","A thrombolytic drug","Amyl nitrite by inhalation","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo treatment is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation: beta blockers are helpful, anticoagulation is added only for a left ventricular thrombus, and diuretics only for heart failure. With neither thrombus nor heart failure, a beta blocker is the helpful medication for her, and most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 69, 103, 104 (slide 69 picture)"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy is found to have cardiac amyloidosis, with amyloid protein deposited in the heart tissue. He has breathlessness, leg edema and ascites. Which electrocardiogram finding is expected with this infiltrative disease?","choices":["Left axis deviation","ST elevation","Low QRS voltage","Prominent Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy depends on the stage and diagnosis, and it may show low QRS voltage in amyloidosis infiltrative disease. Rhythm disorders such as atrial fibrillation and conduction disorders such as bundle branch blocks are also common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy develops a new holosystolic murmur heard best at the apex that radiates to the left axilla. Her ventricle has continued to enlarge on serial echocardiograms. Which complication best accounts for the new murmur?","choices":["Pericardial thickening from constriction","Functional mitral regurgitation from dilation","Outflow obstruction from a thick septum","Tricuspid regurgitation into the axilla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As the ventricle in dilated cardiomyopathy keeps dilating, the valve apparatus is distorted, and mitral regurgitation (which appears earlier) and tricuspid regurgitation commonly develop. Her new holosystolic apical murmur is that mitral regurgitation, and rising end-systolic volumes then drive further dilation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has pulmonary and systemic congestion, with crackles and leg edema. A diuretic is being started. Which statement about diuretic use in her is most accurate?","choices":["Use with caution; stiff ventricles need preload","Contraindicated in any restrictive disease","Give aggressively to fully dry the lungs","Only useful once the ejection fraction falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are used for her pulmonary and systemic congestion, but with caution, because they lower preload and noncompliant ventricles depend on preload to maintain cardiac output. Beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem may also help by lengthening left ventricular filling time. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman developed chest pain and dyspnea during a hurricane evacuation. She has ST elevation, an elevated troponin and B-type natriuretic peptide, and an echocardiogram showing akinesis of the left ventricular apex. Which test confirms stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Cardiac catheterization","Endomyocardial biopsy","Exercise stress test","Serial troponin levels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her stressor (a hurricane evacuation), chest pain, ST elevation, raised troponin and B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP), and apical akinesis all fit stress cardiomyopathy but also mimic acute coronary syndrome. The diagnosis is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy takes a beta blocker, an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a diuretic. His clinician mentions that digoxin may be added later. Which statement describes the place of digoxin in his treatment?","choices":["The first-line drug to start now","A drug to avoid in this disease","A replacement for his diuretic","A second-line drug, not first-line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin is on the list of drugs used to manage dilated cardiomyopathy but is considered a second-line drug. The rest of the list includes angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, beta-adrenergic blockers, angiotensin receptor blockers, spironolactone, sacubitril/valsartan, nitrates, diuretics, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulants. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has had an episode of fainting during basketball practice and brief runs of ventricular tachycardia on monitoring. She wants to keep playing on her competitive school team. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Activity restriction given her high risk","Unrestricted play with extra fluids","Clearance once the murmur softens","Competitive play on a beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her syncope during basketball identifies a patient at high risk of sudden death, and sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has its highest incidence in preadolescents and adolescents and is related to extreme exertion. Activity restriction for high-risk individuals is the adolescent strategy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 65, 66, 69, 76, 82, 105"}]};
